#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049007 :topic/id #uuid "d2f7b6dc-5c4f-5fe0-a707-ef07a4daca20" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147453 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147390 :question/id #uuid "d1946905-5833-58c2-9a0a-4dfd04eb922b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8963" :question/problem "The substitution $z=y^m$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form $z'(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = x^3, \\qquad q(x) = 1, \\qquad n=-\\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the differential equation by $y^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\ny^{1/2} \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y^{3/2}= 1.\\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} =  y^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = \\dfrac{3}{2}y^{1/2} \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny^{1/2}\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(\\ast)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + x^3\\,z = 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{3}{2}x^3z = \\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dc2192f-24a4-5160-844c-4d17096779e5" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Bernoulli Differential Equation to First Order Linear"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147454 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147391 :question/id #uuid "047ea3c1-8ebf-5e26-9f28-c5a87e7cf997" :question/math-academy-id "e-8965" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{y}{x}  = \\dfrac{x^2}{y} .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad q(x) = x^2, \\qquad n=-1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by $y^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\ny \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + \\dfrac{y^{2}}{x}= x^2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} =  y^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 2y \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{1}{x}z  = x^2\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{2}{x} z\n\t\t=2x^2\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=2{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}z) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (2{x}^{2}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =2{x}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{2}) & =2{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{x}^{2} & =\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{5}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2{x}^{5}+C}{5{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5C_1.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z=y^{2}.$ This results in the general solution\n\n$$\ny^{ 2} =\\dfrac{2x^5 + C}{5x^2}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Bernoulli Differential Equation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147456 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147393 :question/id #uuid "b5189ec2-ea1c-5036-9543-b963bd072ab7" :question/math-academy-id "e-9069" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- $y\\,y' + x^2y^2 = e^x y^4$\n\n- $xy' + x^3y = -x^2 y^3$\n\n- $y' + y\\cos{x} = e^{y+1}$" :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\ny' + x^2y = e^x y^3\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) = e^x, \\qquad n=3.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\ny' + x^2y = -xy^3\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) =-x, \\qquad n=3.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $q(x)y^n$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eef27d2b-6149-5070-b711-f4dcc7db2a03" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bernoulli Differential Equations"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2522 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048537}] :topic/title "Bernoulli Differential Equations"}]}
